        "geology": "Part of Zuluand coastal Aquifer (see SAEON MCP site)\r\nThe Lake St Lucia catchment (Figure 1) lies in fundamentally different geological settings that have very different influences on the short and long term survival of the system.",
        "hydrology": "Surface water ground water driven system\r\n While the secondary porosity aquifers  provide the largest volume of river runoff to the lake, it has been established from analyses of historical data that the ecological resilience of the lake is critically dependent on the primary porosity aquifers that provide perennial seepage flows that sustain micro-habitats along the lake shoreline (Taylor, et. al., 2006). In the primary aquifer, the synergy between the groundwater system and coastal lakes is a feature that is common along the east coast of Africa. These coastal lakes and surrounding wetlands are systems where the groundwater fluxes continually move through their shorelines to create groundwater-dependent  ecosystems. The low lying topography in the coastal plains means that the groundwater is generally close to the topographical surface, creating a shallow phreatic zone that strongly influences the terrestrial  ecology. ",
        "soils": "mostly sandy",
        "vegetation": "Withing the Idian Oceaan coastal belt biome, with  natural grassland, coastal forested dune cordon, artifical plantations, forest patches, wetlands and flood plains\r\n"
      },
